 Q2AieN0QhlA,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,How $100 a Month Could Change Your Future,39434,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'How $100 a Month Could Change Your Future' is a concrete-amount, open-outcome title: a small, relatable dollar figure ($100), a recurring cadence (a Month), and a soft, hedged payoff ('Could Change Your Future'). The amount is concrete but the outcome is vague: no end number, no time horizon, no 'math' or 'millionaire' cue, and the modal 'Could' lowers the stakes. Same-channel recurring-contribution and compounding titles split on exactly this dimension. The closest topic twin, vT7IF16WTNg 'Why Everything Changes Once You Invest $100 Per Month', is 3.80373x STRONG; it keeps the $100 figure but adds the channel's 'Why Everything Changes' threshold frame and the verb 'Invest', which tells the viewer the mechanism. Fzdn89C2YXo 'How $500 a Month Makes You a Millionaire (The Surprising Math)' is 3.67806x STRONG with the same 'How $X a Month' opening but a definite verb ('Makes') and a concrete end state ('Millionaire') plus a math parenthetical; hvcr7gVbcr0 '$100,000 Means You're 1/3 Of The Way To $1,000,000 (The Surprising Math)', a contemporaneous age peer, is 2.85698x STRONG; nWkjUETbkkk 'Your Life at Every Level of Compound Interest' 2.03193x STRONG and EjzQXyyREgE 'This is Why Compounding Feels Slow (Until the 7-5-3-1 Rule Kicks In)' 3.43406x STRONG use level/rule structures. Counterexamples that, like this title, promise generic small-amount benefits without a destination number land TYPICAL: 4D90YRoT2yo 'Small Monthly Wins That Can Build $100K' 0.69287x, si24Q4xpT_Y 'Why Small Money Wins Lead to Big Wealth' 0.79722x, tzsM2f6dsEE 'Atomic Money Habits: Small Changes, Compound Results' 0.6133x. Within these eight titles, a definite outcome or explicit math/threshold device co-occurs with STRONG and hedged or generic outcomes with TYPICAL; this is a descriptive pattern, not a cause. Caveat on the twin: vT7IF16WTNg was published 2026-05-19, nine months later, has fewer absolute views (27,553 at about 114 days versus 39,434 at about 398 days), and is ratioed against a different, lower-view peer window, so the 3.80x versus 0.62x gap is relative to channel state at each date, not a clean title A/B.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:Q2AieN0QhlA"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:Q2AieN0QhlA"", ""audit-batches/NICK_147_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Moderate explicit intent: '$100 a month investing' and 'how much will $100 a month grow' are plausible search queries, but the title does not contain 'invest' or a result number. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Aligned on the core promise: the script does show what $100 a month could become ($18,000 after 10 years, over $350,000 after 40 years at 8%, 'well over half a million' at 10%). But roughly a quarter of the text is generic habit, discipline and 'opportunity' material that would fit any savings video, and the numeric payoff is concentrated in about 3,600 characters (7,629 to 11,252). 'Change Your Future' is paid off mostly as mindset rather than a concrete plan (no account type, fund, fee or tax discussion).",,"[""vT7IF16WTNg"", ""Fzdn89C2YXo"", ""nWkjUETbkkk"", ""EjzQXyyREgE"", ""4D90YRoT2yo"", ""si24Q4xpT_Y"", ""tzsM2f6dsEE"", ""hvcr7gVbcr0""]",
 QlPqXGV491Q,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,WTF Is Happening To The Stock Market?!,8054,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'WTF Is Happening To The Stock Market?!' is the channel's 'WTF ... ?!' alarm template applied to the broadest possible object (the stock market). It carries profanity-as-urgency, a present-progressive news frame ('Is Happening') and double punctuation, but no number, ticker, date or named event, so the viewer cannot tell whether the video is about a crash, a rally or a valuation warning. In fact the script describes markets at record highs and argues about concentration and valuation risk, so the title implies a dramatic present event that the script does not report. Same-channel template siblings show the template alone does not predict outcome: f6HIYQUiMoU 'WTF Just Happened To The Housing Market?!' 33.03103x STRONG, but yKaD-ExI7tM 'WTF Just Happened To The American Middle Class?!' 0.87766x TYPICAL (four days earlier) and HJ5GZ2R3n2I 'WTF Just Happened To Your Retirement Accounts?!' 0.94962x TYPICAL. Market-risk siblings sit in a narrow TYPICAL band: qUcdSBhm9us 'If the World is F*cked... Why Are Stocks At Record Highs?' 1.26190x TYPICAL (closest topical match, near-identical ratio), y7DA7evC5WY 'How The Wealthy Invest Before A Market Downturn' 1.27569x TYPICAL, 1W9dzLgpU4k 'What The Average Person Should Do Before The Market Crash' 0.54346x TYPICAL, TIrIi752GSc 'The Bond Market Is Breaking' 0.61547x TYPICAL, and the explainer uSZ1N2ZAmDM 'Dividends 101' 0.31880x WEAK. Within this sample, stock-market alarm titles cluster around 0.5x to 1.3x and none reaches STRONG; the one STRONG template use concerns housing. Descriptive pattern across eight titles, not a proven cause.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QlPqXGV491Q"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QlPqXGV491Q"", ""audit-batches/NICK_148_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Some news-driven query intent ('what is happening to the stock market') plausibly exists around volatile weeks, but the title names no event that would match a specific query; mostly browse/suggested. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Partially aligned. The script does explain 'what is happening' (concentration, valuations, AI capex, inflation, Fed, yields, credit stress, investor positioning), but it explicitly says the market is 'sitting near an all-time high' and that 'this isn't a story about whether the market is going to crash'. The title's alarm and the thumbnail's crash framing promise a decline that has not happened in the script's own account. The script's real thesis, 'you own seven stocks and you don't know it yet', is absent from the title.",,"[""f6HIYQUiMoU"", ""yKaD-ExI7tM"", ""HJ5GZ2R3n2I"", ""qUcdSBhm9us"", ""y7DA7evC5WY"", ""1W9dzLgpU4k"", ""TIrIi752GSc"", ""uSZ1N2ZAmDM""]",
 QogQUuyFUx8,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,"Live Off $500,000 In The Bank And Do Nothing Else",190887,STRONG,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'Live Off $500,000 In The Bank And Do Nothing Else' is an imperative lifestyle promise built on one concrete, round, reachable-sounding dollar figure ($500,000), a deliberately low-tech location ('In The Bank', not 'invested') and an extreme-ease payoff ('Do Nothing Else'). The number undercuts the common $1M-plus retirement benchmark, so the title implies a contrarian 'you need far less than you think' answer without stating it. It is a pure promise with no question, no caveat and no 'math' tag. Same-channel comparisons show the dollar figure alone does not predict outcome: kVpJ1Elw680 'Retire on $1,000,000? The Shocking Reality' (eight days later) 0.15245x WEAK frames a bigger number as a warning/question; HDBlb-oaXeM 'The $2M-$3M Early Retirement Trap Nobody Warns You About' 0.74375x TYPICAL is also a threat frame; jpD1YXj0JRM 'Why Everything Changes After You Hit $500,000 Invested' 1.54694x TYPICAL uses the same number as a milestone rather than an exit; UfqNzF7sQzo 'Why Everything Changes After $3,000 Month Passive Income' 1.6745x TYPICAL. Income-yield titles are split: BL1eTZxWc4A 'How Much You Need to Make $1,000 Month in Dividends?' 4.15859x STRONG versus LJM5qILr3SE 'What $1,000,000 Really Pays in Dividends' 0.55686x TYPICAL and zppYzhlGiOc 'What $100K Invested Really Pays in Dividends (Realistically)' 0.46591x WEAK; uXkF1LtRZso 'How To Retire Early With One House' 0.21768x WEAK. In this sample the strongest titles promise an attainable outcome (live off, make $1,000 a month) while 'reality/realistically/trap' framings of similar money sizes are weaker; this is a descriptive pattern across nine titles, not a proven cause.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QogQUuyFUx8"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QogQUuyFUx8"", ""audit-batches/NICK_149_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Moderate explicit intent ('live off $500,000', 'can I retire on 500k', 'how much interest on 500k') exists as a recognizable query family, but the 'do nothing else' phrasing is browse/aspiration framing. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Partially aligned and partly contradicted. The script does open on the exact promise ('live comfortably with just half a million dollars in the bank') and computes income on $500,000. But 'In The Bank' is quickly replaced: the 5% case uses 'bonds, high yield savings accounts, and other low-risk options', and the main $42,500 case uses a stock/bond portfolio at 8.5%, which is not money in the bank. 'Do Nothing Else' is also undercut: more than half the script is about saving $2,000 a month for 18 years, cutting spending, and discipline, and it adds Social Security as an extra income source. The title promises an endpoint; the script mostly sells the accumulation mindset.",,"[""kVpJ1Elw680"", ""jpD1YXj0JRM"", ""HDBlb-oaXeM"", ""UfqNzF7sQzo"", ""BL1eTZxWc4A"", ""LJM5qILr3SE"", ""zppYzhlGiOc"", ""uXkF1LtRZso""]",
