 Q2AieN0QhlA,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,How $100 a Month Could Change Your Future,39434,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,FULL_TRANSCRIPT_TOPIC,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:Q2AieN0QhlA"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:Q2AieN0QhlA"", ""audit-batches/NICK_147_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Beginner investing: small recurring contributions plus long-horizon compounding, with a habit/discipline layer. Evergreen; very broad household relevance (the entry amount is deliberately low). Mechanisms: dollar-cost contributions, compound growth, return-on-balance scaling, start-age effect, contribution escalation, pay-yourself-first automation. Distinctive audience question: 'Is $100 a month even worth investing?' The video answers yes with correct nominal arithmetic but omits inflation, fees, taxes and account choice, so the answer is motivational rather than actionable.","Original opportunity: keep the $100 entry point but show the full honest table (7%, 8%, 10% nominal; the same in today's dollars; effect of a 0.5% fee; start at 25 versus 35) and a concrete first-step path (where to put $100, automatic transfer). Use a definite destination in packaging (for example the inflation-adjusted figure as a surprise reversal, or the 40-year nominal figure) rather than 'Could Change Your Future'. Avoid the unsourced 'ahead of 60%' and the millionaire teacher story, which the math contradicts.","Same-channel contribution/compounding siblings: vT7IF16WTNg 'Why Everything Changes Once You Invest $100 Per Month' 3.80373x STRONG; Fzdn89C2YXo '$500 a Month Makes You a Millionaire (The Surprising Math)' 3.67806x STRONG; EjzQXyyREgE 7-5-3-1 rule 3.43406x STRONG; nWkjUETbkkk every level of compound interest 2.03193x STRONG; hvcr7gVbcr0 '$100,000 Means You're 1/3 Of The Way' 2.85698x STRONG; generic small-wins siblings 4D90YRoT2yo 0.69287x, si24Q4xpT_Y 0.79722x, tzsM2f6dsEE 0.6133x, all TYPICAL. External competitor coverage not sampled in this review.",,,,
 QlPqXGV491Q,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,WTF Is Happening To The Stock Market?!,8054,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,FULL_TRANSCRIPT_TOPIC,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QlPqXGV491Q"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QlPqXGV491Q"", ""audit-batches/NICK_148_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Time-sensitive stock-market risk commentary: index concentration in mega-cap tech, valuation extremes, AI capex circularity, sticky inflation and a hawkish Fed transition, long-bond yields, credit 'cockroaches', and defensive positioning by famous investors, closed with conventional diversification advice. Audience: passive index-fund and 401k investors, beginner to intermediate. Distinctive audience question: 'Is my boring index fund actually safe if AI stocks fall?' The answer is concrete (check your holdings, rebalance, equal weight, international, bonds, cash) but the evidence half is news-dense and perishable.","Original opportunity: build a durable explainer around the strongest original idea, 'you own seven stocks and you don't know it yet', using dated, sourced index-weight data, a worked example of what a 50% Nvidia drop does to a $100,000 S&P 500 fund given its actual weight, and an equal-weight comparison. Package honestly around concentration rather than a crash that has not happened, and date-stamp every macro statistic.","Same-channel market-risk siblings sit in TYPICAL: qUcdSBhm9us 1.26190x, y7DA7evC5WY 1.27569x, 1W9dzLgpU4k 0.54346x, TIrIi752GSc 0.61547x, HJ5GZ2R3n2I 0.94962x; the 'WTF' template's only STRONG use is housing (f6HIYQUiMoU 33.03103x). External competitor coverage not sampled in this review.",,,,
 QogQUuyFUx8,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,"Live Off $500,000 In The Bank And Do Nothing Else",190887,STRONG,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,FULL_TRANSCRIPT_TOPIC,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QogQUuyFUx8"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QogQUuyFUx8"", ""audit-batches/NICK_149_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Early retirement / financial independence on a specific sub-million number, framed as living off returns forever. Evergreen; beginner-to-intermediate audience; high household relevance because $500,000 is a reachable-sounding target compared with the $1M-plus norm. Mechanisms invoked: interest income, portfolio returns, withdrawal rates, inflation, sequence risk (asserted away), Social Security, expense control, savings rate. Distinctive audience question: 'Is $500,000 enough to stop working, and how much income does it really produce?' The video answers with optimistic yield and return assumptions and then pivots to frugality and mindset, so the core numeric question is only half answered and partly wrong.","Original opportunity: answer the same question with a transparent income ladder for $500,000 using sourced rates as of the upload date (Treasury bills, high-yield savings, a 3.7-4% safe withdrawal rate, an aggressive 6-7% case), showing after-tax income, real income after 10/20/30 years at 3% inflation, and the years-to-depletion table. Add the honest gaps (health insurance before 65, Social Security not available before 62, sequence risk) and keep the endpoint-promise packaging that this video used. Avoid presenting average returns as spendable income and avoid 'financially immortal' claims.","Same-channel number-threshold retirement/income siblings: kVpJ1Elw680 $1M shocking reality 0.15245x WEAK; HDBlb-oaXeM $2M-$3M trap 0.74375x TYPICAL; jpD1YXj0JRM after $500,000 invested 1.54694x TYPICAL; UfqNzF7sQzo $3,000 month passive income 1.6745x TYPICAL; BL1eTZxWc4A $1,000 month dividends 4.15859x STRONG; LJM5qILr3SE $1M dividends 0.55686x TYPICAL; zppYzhlGiOc $100K dividends 0.46591x WEAK; uXkF1LtRZso retire early with one house 0.21768x WEAK. External competitor coverage not sampled in this review.",,,,
