 years': consistent with secondary coverage relaying the BLS April 2026 CPI release of May 12, 2026 (3.8% all items, highest since May 2023, about three years; BLS page itself not opened). Available before the 2026-05-21 publication."", ""'they voted to hold rates steady and the vote was eight to four. The Fed publicly argued with itself for the first time since 1992': secondary coverage of the April 29, 2026 FOMC reports an 8-4 statement vote with four dissents (Miran for a cut; Hammack, Kashkari and Logan against the easing bias), described as the most dissents since 1992. Coverage also reports the rate decision itself as 11-1. 'First time since 1992' overstates: dissents are routine; the narrower claim is four dissents, the most since 1992. Federal Reserve statement not opened."", ""'Jerome Powell ... his term ended in May. He's been replaced by Kevin Warsh': secondary coverage says Powell's chair term ended May 15, 2026 and Warsh had cleared the Senate Banking Committee 13-11; whether Warsh was confirmed and sworn in by the 2026-05-21 publication was not verified here (UNVERIFIED as stated)."", ""'the 30-year Treasury yield just hit 5.19%. The highest level in roughly 19 years': only low-quality mirror sites dated May 19, 2026 reporting a move above 5.19%, 'levels last seen before the 2008 financial crisis' were found; Treasury daily yield-curve data not opened. Directionally plausible but UNVERIFIED against an official series."", ""'Berkshire is sitting on a record 397 billion dollars in cash': supported by Bloomberg Law and other coverage of Berkshire's Q1 2026 report (about $397.4 billion cash and Treasury bills, record, first quarter under CEO Greg Abel), available before publication. 'Just stepped down as CEO' is loose: Abel was CEO for the full quarter. 'That is more than 30% of their total assets' implies total assets under about $1.32 trillion (analyst calculation, 397/0.30); not checked against the balance sheet. The 'I don't see the ideal investing environment' CNBC quote was not verified."", ""'MIT recently published a study looking at 300 real-world corporate AI deployments ... 95% ... zero return ... Not low return. Zero': the MIT NANDA 'GenAI Divide' report (July/August 2025, about nine months before publication, so 'recently' is a stretch) reviewed 300+ publicly disclosed initiatives plus 52 interviews and 153 survey responses; its 95% figure measures pilots that did not reach production with measurable P&L impact within about six months (secondary coverage; report PDF not opened). 'Zero return' and 'not low return' overstate a narrow pilot-to-production metric."", ""'in 2016, those seven stocks were about 12% of the index. Today, they're 35%. The math has nearly tripled': 35/12 = 2.92x (analyst calculation), arithmetically consistent with 'nearly tripled'. Both index weights are UNVERIFIED here."", ""'$725 billion ... That's a 77% increase from last year': implies prior-year spending of about $410 billion (analyst calculation, 725/1.77). 'More than the entire annual GDP of Sweden' not checked against IMF/World Bank data; UNVERIFIED."", ""'OpenAI has committed to roughly $1.4 trillion in spending over the next 8 years. Their current revenue uh about 13 billion ... like signing a 30-year lease on a $4 million mansion while making $36,000 a year': commitments/revenue = about 108x; $4 million/$36,000 = about 111x (analyst calculations), so the analogy matches the total-to-annual-income ratio, but it stretches 8 years to 30 years, which makes the annual burden look smaller (OpenAI: about $175 billion per year vs $13 billion revenue, about 13.5x; mansion: about $133,333 per year vs $36,000, about 3.7x). The underlying figures were not verified."", ""'You might own more Nvidia than you do of 400 of the other companies in your diversified fund combined': hedged ('might') and UNVERIFIED; by the script's own numbers the top ten are about 38%, leaving about 62% across 490 names, so whether the bottom 400 combined weigh less than Nvidia alone depends on index weights not checked here."", ""'Today in May 2026, it's almost 40 ... only time in 145 years ... higher was December 1999 ... in 1929 ... 32', market cap to GDP '231%', price-to-book 'record 5.3', margin debt '$1.3 trillion', debt interest 'over 1 trillion' exceeding military spending, Hussman '63%', 'Nasdaq 130% rally', Bank of America '53%' and the Burry, Grantham, Druckenmiller and Dimon quotes: not checked in this review (search budget); UNVERIFIED. 1880 to 2026 is about 146 years, consistent with '145 years'. Several items (Tricolor, First Brands, Zions/Western Alliance, Dimon's cockroach remark, the November fund manager survey) date from autumn 2025, not 'the last 6 months' relative to May 2026 in every case."", ""'Cash currently pays you almost 4% risk-free': secondary FOMC coverage puts the target range at 3.50% to 3.75% after April 29, 2026, so cash-like yields near 3.5% to 3.7% fit 'almost 4%' loosely; not checked against T-bill rates."", ""'Neuroscience is brutal on this one. People do not sell into euphoria': no study cited; narrator opinion."", ""Possible ASR artefacts preserved, not corrected against audio: 'needed to to that contract', 'If there's any point in If there's any point', 'Not 6%? 63?', 'Open AI', and filler tokens 'um', 'uh'.""], ""ending_words"": ""Just a reminder, I am not a financial advisor. This video is for educational purposes only. Any decisions you make are entirely your own. I'll see you in the next one."", ""opening_words"": ""Two people check their 401k this morning. The first one, let's call him your coworker who won't shut up about it, is up 92% this year. He owns Nvidia."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_STORED_TEXT"", ""source"": {""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::108_QlPqXGV491Q_WTF_Is_Happening_To_The_Stock_M