fore the narrator introduces himself at about 906 characters. A self-diagnosis checklist ('Do you have a target date fund with a year printed in the name? ... a pension?') converts an institutional topic into a personal one, which the title does not do. The single organizing idea is stated explicitly and early: 'Everything you own has two prices' (appraisal versus what someone will hand you this afternoon), and every later section is tied back to it ('Remember your two prices'), so the explainer has a spine rather than a list. Transitions work through callbacks: the windshield-wiper company planted at about 3,600 ('Hold that thought') pays off as First Brands at about 4,100; Alvin's queue is returned to after each mechanism. Analogies are concrete and household (trade-in pricing, house appraisal versus your street, a smoke detector with the battery out, telling a spouse the money is 'fine, just not available'). A strong structural choice is the steel-man section ('let me argue against myself'), which concedes the index returns, locked-fund structure and Blackstone's gate argument, then turns it into a sharper point: the gate 'moves the loss onto whoever needs their money most urgently'. Weaknesses: (1) many figures are given without attribution or date (12% average request, $15 billion, 53%, 8% PIK share, 2.8% non-accruals, 13% of bank lending), so viewers cannot check them; (2) the Alvin/Karina/Erica stories are presented as real ('That happened to a retired machinist named Alvin') with no sourcing, so they should be treated as illustrative and UNVERIFIED; (3) the '5% cap means a 5-year exit' arithmetic is a linear simplification (see claim audit); (4) two mid-video calls to action (subscribe 'without a commission', a school community link) interrupt but are short. The payoff is practical and proportionate: read repurchase terms now, plan cash needs around a 5% cap, and treat a smooth line as absence of pricing rather than safety. All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification."", ""audio_verified"": false, ""availability"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""beat_map"": [{""beat"": ""Cold open: 11 years of smooth gains, asks for $80,000 in March, receives about $40,000 three months later; 'nothing was stolen'; both numbers 'real'"", ""character_range"": [0, 906]}, {""beat"": ""Nick intro, private credit as about $1.7 trillion outside banks and markets, subscribe line, self-diagnosis checklist, introduces Karina and Erica"", ""character_range"": [906, 1984]}, {""beat"": ""Thesis: 'Everything you own has two prices' (appraisal versus cash-out price) with house and stock analogies"", ""character_range"": [1984, 2570]}, {""beat"": ""History: post-2008 rules push loans from banks to funds; 'a bank with better branding and worse disclosure'; windshield-wiper plant; about 30% of below-investment-grade debt, up from 13"", ""character_range"": [2570, 3995]}, {""beat"": ""Trigger events: First Brands (>$10 billion liabilities, about $2.3 billion receivables missing, loan to 33 cents) and Tricolor (about $1.9 billion), JPMorgan $170 million write-off, Dimon 'cockroach' quote"", ""character_range"": [3995, 5489]}, {""beat"": ""Redemption mechanics: retail funds from $2,500, quarterly repurchase offers, 5% cap with proration, 12% average requests, $15 billion asked and about 53% paid, Blue Owl 22% and tech fund over 40%, '5 years to leave', Karina's enrollment email, Erica as system not villain"", ""character_range"": [5489, 8063]}, {""beat"": ""Two prices in practice: Blue Owl merger exchange ratio near 80 cents, deal collapses in 48 hours; listed BDCs about 25% below book, Prospect Capital near 57% discount"", ""character_range"": [8063, 9481]}, {""beat"": ""Cliff Asness 'volatility laundering'; smoothing only postpones bad news because a loan has no upside surprise"", ""character_range"": [9481, 10193]}, {""beat"": ""Emotional texture of a redemption queue: portal every 90 days, $18,000 roof quote versus $201,000 balance, reassuring a spouse; Alvin doing math in retirement"", ""character_range"": [10193, 11036]}, {""beat"": ""Three tells to check tonight: PIK interest (about 8% of income, tripled at an Apollo vehicle), non-accruals 2.8% across 20 largest funds, extend-and-pretend; Fitch default rate over 9% versus sub-1% brochure numbers"", ""character_range"": [11036, 12781]}, {""beat"": ""Retirement-plan channel: August executive order, March DOL proposed safe harbor, over $12 trillion in accounts, fee comparison 3.3% versus 3-20 basis points"", ""character_range"": [12781, 13953]}, {""beat"": ""Five-year comparison: $100,000 to about $181,000 in total US stock market, $156,000 in a better private credit fund, $122,000 in high-yield bonds; school community link"", ""character_range"": [13953, 14454]}, {""beat"": ""Steel-man: direct lending index 9.3% last year, about 9.5% over 20 years, one negative year (2008), losses under 1%; locked 10-year funds; John Gray 'gates are a feature'; BREIT 16-month gate; gate shifts loss to the urgent seller"", ""character_range"": [14454, 16347]}, {""beat"": ""Banks finance the lenders (13% of bank lending, up from 4% in 2015); disclaimer; 'You are not stupid' reassurance for Alvin and the viewer"", ""character_range"": [16347, 17500]}, {""beat"": ""Calm action list: read repurchase terms tonight, assume a 5-year exit under stress, stop reading smooth lines as safety; restated balanced thesis; Alvin's statement 'stays beautiful'; community CTA"", ""character_range"": [17500, 18570]}, {""beat"": ""Closing rule: ask what somebody would hand you for it this afternoon"", ""character_range"": [18570, 18792]}], ""claim_audit"": [""'Then last September, two companies collapsed 19 days apart ... First Brands Group ... Tricolor': both collapses occurred in September 2025, before the 2026-08-21 publication. Secondary coverage (CNN syndication, Cambridge Associates) confirms Tricolor's September bankruptcy and First Brands' Chapter 11 'weeks later'. Commonly reported dates (Tricolor about 10 September 2025, First Brands about 28 September 2025) would give 18 days, not 19, but the exact dates were not confirmed in this review's searches, so the 19-day figure is UNVERIFIED. The 'about $2.3 billion' figure matches reporting of a court filing; '$10 billion in liabilities', 'inflated 50 times' and '33 cents' were not checked."", ""'JPMorgan wrote off $170 million ... When you see one cockroach, there are probably more': confirmed by secondary coverage of JPMorgan's Q3 2025 earnings call (October 2025); JPMorgan's $170 million charge was tied to Tricolor. The narrator's phrase 'the SEC now says substantially did not exist' was not checked against an SEC filing; UNVERIFIED."", ""'This year, across the 12 largest of these funds, the average request to leave hit 12%. Investors like you asked for $15 billion and got back about 53% of it': secondary/trade coverage reports total non-traded BDC redemption requests of more than $15 billion in Q1 2026 (Robert A. Stanger and Co. data) with about $7 billion returned, roughly 47% (analyst calculation 7/15), and one aggregator cites a 53% Q1 fulfilment rate; Q2 2026 requests were reported at about $15.6 billion with $5.9 billion paid (about 38%). Fitch reported Q3 requests averaging 9.8% across 13 of 15 perpetual BDCs, down from 10.3% in Q2 (Q3 data post-dates publication). The narrator's figures are broadly consistent with Q1 2026 but the '12 largest' and '12%' framing was not matched to a named source; treat as PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED. Primary Stanger data not opened."", ""'You get 5% a quarter, so it takes you 5 years to leave': 20 quarters x 5% = 100% only if each quarter you receive 5% of your original holding. If the fund repurchases 5% of shares outstanding and every holder requests 100%, each holder receives 5% of the remaining balance each quarter, so after 20 quarters about 35.8% is still unredeemed (0.95^20 = 0.358, analyst calculation) and full exit never mathematically completes. If only a minority request, exit is faster. The narrator correctly says this is 'the flattering version', but the 5-year figure is a simplification, not a ceiling."", ""'Alvin asked for 80,000 and got half': $40,000 / $80,000 = 50% (analyst calculation), consistent with the stated 53% average fulfilment. Alvin, Karina and Erica are presented as real people with no sourcing; UNVERIFIED and likely composite or illustrative."", ""'Last November, Blue Owl proposed merging two of its own funds ... roughly 80 cents on the dollar ... The deal collapsed in 48 hours': the OBDC/OBDC II merger was terminated effective 18 November 2025 and announced 19 November 2025 (SEC 8-K exhibit; trade coverage), citing market conditions, with reports that OBDC II holders faced a redemption pause and potential losses from OBDC trading below NAV. The 'roughly 80 cents' exchange-ratio estimate and the '48 hours' timing were not confirmed in this review; the merger had been announced earlier in November per secondary coverage. Later '$1.4 billion of assets ... wind the fund down' UNVERIFIED."", ""'One Prospect Capital disclosed a discount near 57%. ... offering you 43 cents': arithmetic consistent (1 - 0.57 = 0.43). The discount figure and the group-wide '25% below stated book value' were not checked; UNVERIFIED."", ""'Last August, an executive order told the Labor Department ... and this March, it proposed the rule handing your employer a safe harbor': confirmed. Executive Order 14330, signed 7 August 2025; DOL proposed 'Fiduciary Duties in Selecting Designated Investment Alternatives' on 30 March 2026 with comments due 1 June 2026 (law-firm summaries: Morrison Foerster, Gibson Dunn, Sullivan and Cromwell). Note the proposal is described as asset-neutral and process-based; it does not mandate private credit, so 'it arrives inside your target date fund' is a plausible pathway, not a rule requirement."", ""'You pay around 3.3% a year on the retail version. You would pay three to 20 basis points for an index fund. That is three percentage points of drag': 3.30% minus 0.20% = 3.10 points; minus 0.03% = 3.27 points (analyst calculation), so 'three percentage points' is consistent with the narrator's own inputs. The 3.3% total-cost figure is UNVERIFIED and varies by fund and share class (management, incentive, servicing and interest expense are often combined in such totals)."", ""'A hundred thousand dollars five years ago is about a hundred eighty-one thousand today in the total United States stock market. ... 156 ... 122': implied annualized returns are about 12.6%, 9.3% and 4.1% respectively (analyst calculation). The underlying index and fund return series were not fetched; UNVERIFIED. Because the period is unspecified ('five years ago' from August 2026) and the private credit fund is unnamed, the comparison cannot be reproduced."", ""'The main direct lending index returned 9.3% last year, has averaged 9 and 1/2 over 20 years, and posted exactly one negative year in 2008': likely refers to the Cliffwater Direct Lending Index; not checked; UNVERIFIED."", ""'Fitch tracked about 300 of these companies and found a default rate over 9% last year'; 'non-accruals ... 2.8% ... highest since 2017'; 'PIK ... around 8% of interest income'; 'close to 30% of all below investment grade corporate debt ... up from 13'; '13% of all bank lending, up from 4% in 2015'; '1.7 trillion dollars': none checked in this review; UNVERIFIED. These are plausible industry statistics but the script cites no source or date for most of them."", ""'In late 2022, Blackstone's real estate version gated for 16 straight months ... by early 2024, it was paying everybody again': BREIT began limiting redemptions in December 2022 and reported fulfilling all requests in early 2024 (reviewer background knowledge; not re-checked in this review), so the timeline is plausible but UNVERIFIED here."", ""Possible ASR artefacts preserved, not corrected against audio: 'And the the gap', 'the answer 