t signposts ('So, the real question is', 'Okay. Take a breath. Because we haven't even gotten to the macro yet', 'here's where I need to re-hook you because we still haven't gotten to the cockroaches'); the self-aware re-hook is unusual and honest about intent, and the delayed 'cockroaches' payoff creates a curiosity gap that is paid within a minute. Analogies make large numbers felt (stocks worth more than the economy as 'a single restaurant being worth more than the entire neighborhood'; OpenAI commitments as a $4 million 30-year lease on a $36,000 salary). Weaknesses: the volume of statistics is high and mostly unsourced on screen, several are from late 2025 rather than 'right now', and the escalation is relentlessly one-sided until a short balance section ('Bears have been bearish since 2023 ... missed a 130% rally', Paul Tudor Jones). The 'keeping score' recap at about 12,046 is effective consolidation before the investor-authority section. The payoff is sensible and modest (rebalance, equal-weight, international, bonds, some cash, do not time the top), which contradicts the alarm level of the title and thumbnail but is consistent with the script's own 'am I telling you to sell everything tomorrow? No.' The 'neuroscience is brutal' line is asserted without a study. Close repeats 'the top is a process, the bottom is a moment' and a comment prompt about top-three 401k holdings, followed by a disclaimer. All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification.", "audio_verified": false, "availability": "TEXT_AVAILABLE", "beat_map": [{"beat": "Two-person 401k hook (coworker up 92% on Nvidia vs you in an S&P 500 fund), 'you own Nvidia too' reversal, Nick intro, subscribe ask, thesis 'you own seven stocks and you don't know it yet'", "character_range": [0, 1369]}, {"beat": "Market at records (S&P near all-time high, Nasdaq records, Dow above 50,000); seven stocks about 35% of index (12% in 2016), top ten about 38%, 42% of 2025 returns, Nvidia a