 ratio 1.60616x TYPICAL, both independently reproduced. The tier is peer-sensitive: it is STRONG against the ten-peer window but only TYPICAL against the five-peer window, so 'breakout' should be stated as conditional on the peer definition. In absolute terms it has the most views of all ten age peers (next highest hvcr7gVbcr0 167,634 and Att5nm-UN8M 151,424), so it is at least among the channel's top results of that month. Likes 4,900 (2.57% of views) and comments 1,183 (0.62%); the comment rate is relatively high, consistent with a debatable claim inviting replies, but comment content was not reviewed. Evidence-backed interpretation: in this month the channel's STRONG peers all offer an attainable, positively framed money outcome ($100,000 fast, 1/3 of the way to $1M, invest or pay off debt, doing better than you think), while the WEAK peers are warnings or 'realistic' downgrades ('Retire on $1,000,000? The Shocking Reality' 0.15245x, 'Money Traps' 0.24125x, '$100K ... Really Pays in Dividends (Realistically)' 0.46591x). This video is the most extreme attainable-outcome promise in the set. Hypotheses: (a) a lower-than-expected target number ($500,000 versus the $1M-plus norm) combined with a 'do nothing' payoff creates a strong click motive for a broad audience; (b) the thumbnail's reclining character visualizes the payoff, adding to the title rather than restating it; (c) the contrarian and arguably wrong claims (living forever off 8.5%) may drive comments and debate; (d) the five-peer result suggests part of the measured outperformance comes from comparison against weaker distant peers. Proposed experiment: in owned uploads, test the same retirement-number question with promise packaging ('Live off $X') versus warning packaging ('Retire on $X? The Shocking Reality'), and test whether accurate withdrawal-rate math in the script changes retention or comment sentiment. None of these hypotheses is measurable here without impressions, CTR, traffic sources or retentio