iguous chunks (0-10,000, 10,000-18,792). This is a markedly different script from the channel's usual list format: a narrative explainer with three recurring characters (Alvin, a retired machinist stuck in a redemption queue; Karina, a 34-year-old whose target-date plan may add private credit; Erica, the advisor 'who is not your villain'). The hook is an inverted-good-news cold open ('gone up every single quarter for 11 years ... which should probably have been your first clue') followed by a concrete paradox (asked for $80,000, received 'a wire ... for 40,000 and change', 'nothing was stolen') that sets up the whole thesis before the narrator introduces himself at about 906 characters. A self-diagnosis checklist ('Do you have a target date fund with a year printed in the name? ... a pension?') converts an institutional topic into a personal one, which the title does not do. The single organizing idea is stated explicitly and early: 'Everything you own has two prices' (appraisal versus what someone will hand you this afternoon), and every later section is tied back to it ('Remember your two prices'), so the explainer has a spine rather than a list. Transitions work through callbacks: the windshield-wiper company planted at about 3,600 ('Hold that thought') pays off as First Brands at about 4,100; Alvin's queue is returned to after each mechanism. Analogies are concrete and household (trade-in pricing, house appraisal versus your street, a smoke detector with the battery out, telling a spouse the money is 'fine, just not available'). A strong structural choice is the steel-man section ('let me argue against myself'), which concedes the index returns, locked-fund structure and Blackstone's gate argument, then turns it into a sharper point: the gate 'moves the loss onto whoever needs their money most urgently'. Weaknesses: (1) many figures are given without attribution or date (12% average request, $15 billion, 53%, 8% PIK share, 2.8% non-accruals, 13% of bank lending)