hen the phone. At 320px 'STILL DROPS?!' stays clearly legible with high black-on-white contrast and the angry expression remains readable; the 'CREDIT SCORE' label on the phone becomes tiny and only marginally readable, while the red down-arrow remains visible as a color accent. So on mobile the topic (credit score) is carried mainly by the title, not the image. The image text does not repeat the title; 'STILL DROPS?!' depends on the title's 'Even When You Pay On Time' to make sense, which is a coherent title-thumbnail split, and the anger matches the script's stated aim that the viewer will be 'genuinely angry at a system'. No number (716, 22 points, $31,200) is shown. Same generic white-background cartoon-plus-arrow template seen on other channel thumbnails; historical continuity, A/B testing and CTR contribution are unverified.", "coverage": {"Association_verified": "NO", "Historical_design": "UNKNOWN", "Local_asset": "", "Mobile_review": "NOT_REVIEWED", "Thumbnail_URL": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/RUVOjfrIemY/maxresdefault.jpg", "URL_status": "PRESENT_NOT_FETCHED", "Video_ID": "RUVOjfrIemY"}, "dimensions": [1280, 720], "historical_design": "UNKNOWN", "image_path": "research/finance-economics-intelligence/audit-batches/NICK_RUVOjfrIemY.jpg", "image_sha256": "9f1aaeba346d164b2780eec6b5e92212a28bd3104d11a2f8d07fb241b5c48250", "image_source": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/RUVOjfrIemY/maxresdefault.jpg", "mobile_review_width": 320, "performance_effect": "NOT_ESTABLISHED", "review_scope": "ACTUAL_IMAGE", "source_refs": ["VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "THUMBNAIL_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json"]}, "title": {"analysis": "'Why Your Credit Score Drops Even When You Pay On Time' is a 'Why Your X' explainer title built on a paradox: the viewer's responsible behavior (paying on time) is paired with an unexpected penalty (score drops). It names a concrete, personally owned object (your credit score) and a common frustration, so it is clearer than the channel's abstract mindset titles. It has no number, dollar figure or stakes marker (the script's strongest payoffs, the 22-point drop and the claimed $31,200 mortgage cost, are absent from the title), and it reads like a search-style FAQ rather than a surprising reveal. Same-channel comparisons: the closest topical sibling, 4eGrsL5CwBQ 'Why Everything Changes After You Get 832 Credit Score', published three days earlier, landed at 0.54383x TYPICAL, so two credit-score titles in one week both sat near the WEAK/TYPICAL boundary. Other 'Why Your [money object]' explainers are also low: J_sTOWazmho 'Why Your $1,000 Car Payment Actually Costs $4,000 (The Hidden Math)' 0.50669x TYPICAL despite a dollar figure; debt how-to r4fpD0dgQ64 'Fast Way to Pay Off $10K in Debt on a Small Salary' 0.2221x WEAK; gwm1Hr5iipU 'The 50 Year Mortgage Trap That'll Keep You in Debt Forever' 0.32481x WEAK. Counterexamples where 'Why Your' worked attach the question to a life-stage or wealth aspiration: mWhpJZrTyNU 'Why Your First Million Feels Impossible (And The Next Comes Fast)' 2.07599x STRONG and ZnI6pKMnpf4 'Why Your 40s Beat Your 20s for Building Wealth' 1.68072x TYPICAL; HEtVmp38D4M 'Invest or Pay Off Debt? The Wealthy Know the Answer' 3.64145x STRONG frames debt as a wealth decision; tynTfEi4518 'The Car Payment Trap Nobody Talks About' 24.51666x STRONG frames a debt object as a hidden trap. Within this small sample, credit/debt remediation framing co-occurs with weak results and aspiration or trap framing with stronger ones; this is descriptive across eight titles, not a proven cause.", "peer_comparison_ids": ["4eGrsL5CwBQ", "J_sTOWazmho", "r4fpD0dgQ64", "gwm1Hr5iipU", "mWhpJZrTyNU", "ZnI6pKMnpf4", "HEtVmp38D4M", "tynTfEi4518"], "review_scope": "ACTUAL_TITLE", "script_alignment": "Well aligned at the start: the cold open is exactly the promised scenario (bills paid in full, score falls 22 points from 738 to 716 on utilization). The script then broadens to seven traps (utilization snapshot, limit cuts, hard inquiries, closing old cards, paying off an installment loan, authorized-user late payments, score-version mismatch) plus report errors and a bureau business-model segment. Some sections (inquiries, report errors, multiple score versions) are not really 'drops while paying on time', so the title undersells the breadth and the Thomas mortgage-cost payoff.", "search_demand": "Plausible explicit search intent ('why did my credit score drop', 'credit score dropped even though I paid on time' are natural queries), so the title is closer to search phrasing than most channel titles. Historical query volume, search traffic share and impressions are unavailable.", "source_refs": ["VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json"]}, "topic": {"analysis": "Credit-score mechanics explainer: why a score falls without missed payments (utilization snapshot timing, limit cuts, hard inquiries, closing old accounts, credit-mix change after paying off a loan, authorized-user exposure, score-version mismatch, report errors). Evergreen, broad household relevance (anyone with a card or planning a mortgage), beginner-to-intermediate depth. The distinctive audience question is 'I did everything right, why did my number fall?' and the practical answer (pay before statement close, keep old cards, check the lender's FICO version, pull all three reports) is concrete and correct in direction. It departs from the channel's dominant mindset and wealth-psychology content toward consumer-credit service content.", "competition": "Same-channel credit/debt service titles sit low: 4eGrsL5CwBQ 832 credit score 0.54383x TYPICAL, J_sTOWazmho car payment hidden math 0.50669x TYPICAL, r4fpD0dgQ64 pay off 10K debt 0.2221x WEAK, gwm1Hr5iipU 50-year mortgage trap 0.32481x WEAK. Aspiration or trap framings do better: mWhpJZrTyNU 2.07599x STRONG, ZnI6pKMnpf4 1.68072x TYPICAL, HEtVmp38D4M 3.64145x STRONG, tynTfEi4518 24.51666x STRONG. External credit-education competitors (bureaus, card-review sites, large finance creators) were not sampled in this review.", "novelty_and_corpus_angle": "Original opportunity: keep the running case-study device (one person, one mortgage meeting, one dollar payoff) but make the arithmetic exact and the case consistent, and anchor each mechanic to the issuer's or FICO's own documentation (statement-date reporting, the 45-day rate-shopping window the script omits, FTC's 5% material-error finding rather than the 40-million extrapolation). A stronger packaging angle may be the dollar consequence ('A 29-point drop cost him $36,000') rather than the mechanism question. Avoid fixed point-loss numbers FICO does not publish.", "review_scope": "FULL_TRANSCRIPT_TOPIC", "source_refs": ["VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json"]}, "virality": {"alternative_explanations": ["Audience-fit hypothesis: credit-score repair content may draw a different, smaller audience than the channel's wealth-mindset core; traffic and audience data unavailable.", "Cannibalization hypothesis: 4eGrsL5CwBQ, another credit-score title three days earlier, may have absorbed demand; impressions unavailable.", "Packaging hypothesis: no number or dollar stakes in title or thumbnail, and the topic label on the phone is illegible on mobile; CTR unavailable.", "Confounders: algorithmic exploration timing, the extreme housing-market outlier f6HIYQUiMoU in the same window, unknown later title or thumbnail edits."], "analysis": "Observed result: 5,344 frozen views at 155.1184 days; ten-peer ratio 0.49765x WEAK and five-peer ratio 0.47287x WEAK, both independently reproduced. The tier agrees across peer windows, but the ten-peer ratio sits just under the 0.5 boundary, so the WEAK label is near the TYPICAL edge. Likes 276 (5.16% of views) and comments 45 (0.84%), engagement rates higher than in several recent channel records reviewed in this series, which is consistent with, but does not prove, satisfied clickers. Evidence-backed interpretation: in its ten-video age window, STRONG peers were a broad net-worth trap list (8rhg_K7c4O8 4.84661x), a housing-market news reaction (f6HIYQUiMoU 33.03103x) and a comparative identity list (ypUI1Sv-Nhk 2.18459x), while the other credit-score title three days earlier (4eGrsL5CwBQ 0.54383x) also landed near the boundary; two credit-score videos in one week may have split the same narrow audience. Hypotheses: (a) a credit-score-remediation topic attracts a narrower, more search-driven audience than the channel's wealth-identity browse audience; (b) the title lacks a number or stakes marker that the channel's STRONG titles often carry; (c) the thumbnail's topic cue ('CREDIT SCORE' on the phone) is nearly illegible at mobile size; (d) audience overlap with 4eGrsL5CwBQ. Proposed experiment: in owned uploads, test the same script under a dollar-stakes package (for example a mortgage-cost title with the number in the thumbnail) against the mechanism-question package, spacing credit topics at least two weeks apart, and measure CTR, impressions, search vs browse traffic share and retention at the Thomas callbacks. None of these hypotheses is measurable here without impressions, CTR, traffic sources or retention.", "causal_status": "DESCRIPTIVE_ASSOCIATION_NOT_CAUSAL", "channel_context": "WEAK in both peer windows in a week where a news reaction and broad wealth lists were STRONG; comparison is within-channel so subscriber base is roughly constant, but topic audience differs between credit service content and wealth-identity content.", "competition": "mErWx9GiVUc 0.77667x TYPICAL; eAzo0uvSnus 0.72657x TYPICAL; zwrY8QsDTgs 1.86362x TYPICAL; 4eGrsL5CwBQ 0.54383x TYPICAL; ypUI1Sv-Nhk 2.18459x STRONG; R_E6OOnF9e4 0.59678x TYPICAL; 8rhg_K7c4O8 4.84661x STRONG; f6HIYQUiMoU 33.03103x STRONG; ccNuYRhrA0U 1.00027x TYPICAL; 8LncpNMNPW8 0.31287x WEAK. Three of ten age peers are STRONG, six TYPICAL, and this video is one of two WEAK in the window alongside 8LncpNMNPW8.", "format_fit": "20:12 long-form explainer with a running case study, labelled trap sections and a five-step action close; cartoon thumbnail; runtime is not watch time.", "observed_result": "5,344 views; 276 likes; 45 comments; 1,212 seconds; 0.49765x WEAK ten-peer / 0.47287x WEAK five-peer. Public metrics only.", "original_content_lesson": {"confidence": "HIGH for mortgage and utilization arithmetic and for the FTC, myFICO inquiry and CFPB enforcement checks; MEDIUM for the within-channel descriptive pattern; LOW for causal virality attribution.", "test": "Produce a credit-mechanics explainer with one consistent case study whose payoff number is exactly computed (about 36,600 dollars for 0.4 points on 380,000 dollars over 30 years), include the rate-shopping window and FTC's 5% material-error finding, and test a dollar-stakes package against a mechanism-question package in owned experiments; measure CTR, search share, and retention at each case-study callback."}, "review_scope": "INDIVIDUAL_PUBLIC_EVIDENCE", "source_refs": ["VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY", "audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json", "PERFORMANCE_SENSITIVITY.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"], "topic_demand": "Evergreen consumer-credit question with plausible steady search demand; historical search volume unavailable.", "trend_timing": "Published 2026-04-08 in a dense daily-upload window; no dated news catalyst is tied to the video (the CFPB actions it cites were from January 2025); Google Trends history not fetched."}}
+{"Channel_ID": "UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w", "Video_ID": "R_E6OOnF9e4", "batch_id": "NICK_154", "event": {"confidence": "UNKNOWN", "linkage": "No dated event or historical search series is linked; evergreen topic.", "source_ids": [], "status": "NOT_RESEARCHED", "trends_status": "UNAVAILABLE"}, "identity": {"Age_days": "151.1351", "Audience_problem": "REQUIRES_SEMANTIC_REVIEW", "Channel": "Nick Invests", "Channel_ID": "UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w", "Classification_method": "TITLE_KEYWORD_HEURISTIC_NOT_SEMANTIC_REVIEW", "Current_views": "NOT_FETCHED", "Dataset": "DS-000022", "Demand_type": "UNVERIFIED", "Duration_seconds": "1415", "Economic_mechanism": "REQUIRES_SEMANTIC_REVIEW", "Evidence_locator": "data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Master_Data.xlsx", "Format": "LONG_FORM", "Format_source_value": "video", "Lifetime_rate_ratio": "0.59678", "Metadata_source": "data/catalog/youtube_channel_registry.db:videos:R_E6OOnF9e4", "Metric_snapshot_date": "2026-09-11", "Narrative_angle": "UNVERIFIED", "Peer_Video_IDs": "ypUI1Sv-Nhk|ccNuYRhrA0U|zwrY8QsDTgs|8LncpNMNPW8|eAzo0uvSnus|vXBhBlBIUDs|RUVOjfrIemY|1W9dzLgpU4k|mErWx9GiVUc|-DIT32sBkAk", "Peer_count": "10", "Performance_tier": "TYPICAL", "Published_at_raw": "2026-04-12 20:45:28", "Snapshot_precision": "SOURCE_DECLARED_SCRAPE_DAY_TIMEZONE_UNKNOWN", "Tier_basis": "DESCRIPTIVE_LIFETIME_RATE", "Time_sensitivity": "UNVERIFIED", "Title": "15 Low Effort Boring Tasks That Make More Than Your Job", "Topic_tags_provisional": "UNCLASSIFIED", "URL": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R_E6OOnF9e4", "Video_ID": "R_E6OOnF9e4", "Views": "6609", "comments": 46, "description": "Join my community here: \nhttps://www.skool.com/nick-invests-co...\n\nDiscover strategies to build long-term wealth without sacrificing your personal life or well-being. This video offers practical insights on creating sustainable financial growth, achieving financial freedom, and avoiding burnout while managing your income streams. Learn how to balance wealth-building, smart investing, passive income, and lifestyle optimization for lasting success and financial independence\n\nDisclaimer: The purpose is to inform viewers about finance in a responsible, educational way, not to provide financial advice. This video is for educational and entertainment purposes only. I am not a financial advisor. Please consult with a professional before making major financial decisions.\n\n#savemoney #personalfinance #investing", "description_unavailable_reason": null, "likes": 289}, "performance": {"Age_days": "151.1351", "Format": "LONG_FORM", "Lifetime_rate_ratio": "0.59678", "Metric_snapshot_date": "2026-09-11", "Peer_Video_IDs": "ypUI1Sv-Nhk|ccNuYRhrA0U|zwrY8QsDTgs|8LncpNMNPW8|eAzo0uvSnus|vXBhBlBIUDs|RUVOjfrIemY|1W9dzLgpU4k|mErWx9GiVUc|-DIT32sBkAk", "Peer_count": "10", "Performance_tier": "TYPICAL", "Tier_basis": "DESCRIPTIVE_LIFETIME_RATE", "evidence_kind": "OBSERVED_FACT", "methodology": "METHODS.md; build_baseline.compare"}, "private_metrics": {"CTR": "UNAVAILABLE_PRIVATE_METRIC", "impressions": "UNAVAILABLE_PRIVATE_METRIC", "retention": "UNAVAILABLE_PRIVATE_METRIC", "revenue": "UNAVAILABLE_PRIVATE_METRIC", "traffic_sources": "UNAVAILABLE_PRIVATE_METRIC"}, "script": {"analysis": "Full 20,047 canonical characters read in three contiguous chunks (0-10,000, 10,000-20,000, 20,000-20,047). The hook is a controlled comparison story: Jude and Mary share neighborhood, school, graduation year, a roughly $58,000 salary and $3,400 monthly take-home, then diverge on the same Tuesday, Jude with '$190 left and 3 weeks until the next paycheck' and Mary with '$1,240 that came in this month' from 'boring, unsexy systems'. This is the strongest writing in the video: it holds every variable constant except the one the video sells, uses concrete dollar amounts and a specific day, and pre-empts the 'he just overspends' objection ('No shopping addiction, no gambling problem, the money just goes'). The intro then makes the promise ('15 of the most boring, low-effort income tasks that can quietly make you more than your job'), softening the title with 'can'. A tax-and-inflation section frames one paycheck as structurally disadvantaged before Mary returns as a bridge ('Here's exactly how she did it'), which converts the thesis into a case study. Items are delivered in a consistent micro-template: what it is, a dollar range, startup cost, time, then an 'honest note' caveat. That template is the script's best technique, because the caveats (vault cash 'literally sitting inside a metal box in someone else's bar', 'absentee ownership' called misleading, only '15 to 25% of designs generate any sales', AI pressure on stock photos) build credibility. Mary is threaded through items 1, 2 and 14 as a running example, and section signposts ('Five down, 10 to go', the pivot 'Everything so far requires space you own or equipment you buy. The next five require neither', '10 down, five left') keep the 15-item length navigable; the pivot is the most effective transition because it explains why the list changes category. The mid-video re-engagement ('Most watch a video like this, feel briefly motivated, and go back') is a standard pattern interrupt that flatters the remaining viewer. Weaknesses: the honest caveats directly undercut the 'low effort' and 'more than your job' promise, so the packaging and the content disagree; several internal numbers contradict each other (vending, print on demand, Mary's monthly total); and the closing Mary ledger quietly requires about $160,000 of invested capital, which a $58,000 earner could not have accumulated in 18 months from salary, so the hook's 'same salary' premise is broken at the payoff. The close returns to the Jude/Mary frame ('Jude is still waiting. Mary already stopped thinking about it'), gives three concrete 15-to-45-minute actions (open a high-yield account, list a parking or storage space, start SCHD or VNQ in a Roth IRA with $50 a month), then a not-financial-advice disclaimer and subscribe call to action; the early subscribe/like ask also appears at about 1,500 characters. No sponsor, affiliate link or paid product appears in the stored text; the video description was not reviewed. All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification.", "audio_verified": false, "availability": "TEXT_AVAILABLE", "beat_map": [{"beat": "Jude and Mary controlled-comparison hook: same school, $58,000 salary, $3,400 take-home; Jude at $190 by the 22nd versus Mary's $1,240 side deposit from parking, storage and dividends; 'not a video about working harder'", "character_range": [0, 1171]}, {"beat": "Nick intro, early subscribe/like ask, promise of '15 of the most boring, low-effort income tasks that can quietly make you more than your job'", "character_range": [1171, 1722]}, {"beat": "Thesis: wage income taxed hardest (7.65% payroll, $42,000 to $46,000 kept on $58,000), capital gains 0 to 20%, Forbes 400 at 8.2%, 'IRS data' seven income streams, essentials up nearly 25% versus wages 21% since 2021", "character_range": [1722, 3130]}, {"beat": "Mary bridge ('18 months later, $1,240 a month'); item 1 parking spot rental (hospital $150 to $220, Austin $360, Boston $1,000+, Mary $195); item 2 storage rental via Neighbor ($160 average, $294 per hour of work claim)", "character_range": [3130, 5378]}, {"beat": "Item 3 vending machines ($300 to $600 per machine, cold calling caveat); item 4 ATM ownership (300 x $3 = $900 gross, vault cash caveat); item 5 self-service car wash (KKR $850 million Quick Quack, 14.6% margins, absentee-ownership caveat); 'Five down, 10 to go'", "character_range": [5378, 8221]}, {"beat": "Category pivot to capital-only income streams; item 6 high-yield savings (0.39% national average, 10 to 12 times more); item 7 T-bills 3.6 to 4.0% and I bonds 4.03%, state-tax exemption", "character_range": [8221, 10266]}, {"beat": "Item 8 SCHD (3.4% yield, 12% 10-year return, $10,000 to $340 to $378 to over $800); item 9 REITs and VNQ (90% distribution rule, 3.85% yield, Roth/401k tax fix); item 10 Groundfloor real estate lending (about 10% since 2013, spread $5,000 across 50 loans)", "character_range": [10266, 12570]}, {"beat": "'10 down, five left' re-engagement; item 11 digital downloads (164,000 Etsy shops, average $2,444 revenue, median profit $489, $110,000 and $93,000 creator examples, 20 to 40 hours a week up front); item 12 print on demand ($1 per design per month, 15 to 25% of designs sell)", "character_range": [12570, 14696]}, {"beat": "Item 13 stock photos and video (AI pressure, video 20 times photo); item 14 paid focus groups (Respondent $75 to $400, UserTesting $10, Prolific $12.50 per hour, Mary $600 for six hours); item 15 laundromat (94.8% five-year survival, $40,000 to $100,000 net, 'Laundromat Millionaire', SBA 10% down)", "character_range": [14696, 17761]}, {"beat": "Mary's 18-month ledger ($195 parking, $190 storage, $40,000 savings earning $1,680, SCHD $340 last quarter, Groundfloor 9.8% across 30 loans, 'just over $1,300 a month'); Jude still at $190; wage income as 'the most expensive income stream'; millionaire seven-streams claim repeated", "character_range": [17761, 19080]}, {"beat": "Three 'this week' actions (high-yield account, list parking or storage, SCHD or VNQ in a Roth IRA at $50 a month); Jude/Mary callback; not-financial-advice disclaimer; subscribe call to action", "character_range": [19080, 20047]}], "claim_audit": ["Title and promise 'make more than your job' versus the script's own numbers: Mary's combined result is 'just over $1,300 a month', about $15,600 a year, against a stated $3,400 monthly take-home, about $40,800 a year (analyst calculation, 38%). Most item ranges (parking $150 to $360, storage $160 to $400, savings interest, focus groups $150 to $900 a month) are a fraction of a $58,000 salary. In FTC terms, presenting outlier examples (Boston host over $1,000 a month, Rachel Jimenez $110,000 in year two, Amoro Designs $93,000 a year, a laundromat owner at about $2 million across four locations) without typical-result context resembles the atypical-earnings pattern that FTC consent orders (for example the Lurn/Singal and Cohen orders) require advertisers to pair with clear disclosure of typical customer results. This video sells no product in its stored text, so FTC rules on sellers do not directly apply; the point is that the claims would not meet that standard. The script partly mitigates this with honest caveats and once gives a median (Etsy median profit $489 versus $2,444 average revenue).", "Mary ledger capital: $40,000 in high-yield savings earning $1,680 a year implies 4.2% APY (analyst calculation, plausible). 'SCHD in her Roth IRA paying $340 in dividends last quarter' at the video's own 3.4% yield implies about $40,000 in SCHD (340 x 4 / 0.034). Known items total about $638 a month (195 + 190 + 140 + 113), so 'just over $1,300' requires about $662 a month from Groundfloor, about $81,000 lent at 9.8%. Total implied capital is about $161,000 (analyst calculation). That is incompatible with the hook's premise of a $58,000 earner with $3,400 take-home building this in 18 months from 'four evenings' of setup, and $40,000 in a Roth IRA in 18 months exceeds IRS annual contribution limits ($7,000 for 2025, $7,500 for 2026; limits stated from analyst knowledge, not re-fetched here) unless funded by rollover or conversion. The opening figure ($1,240 a month) and the closing figure (just over $1,300) also differ, and the closing list omits the dividend deposit and focus-group income in a consistent way. Also the $340 is an annual figure for $10,000 in item 8 but a quarterly figure for Mary, which may be a script slip.", "Vending internal contradiction: 'Average net profit per machine, $300 to $600 a month' but 'A 10-machine operation produces $500 to $1,200 a month'. Ten machines at the stated per-machine figure would be $3,000 to $6,000 a month (analyst calculation); the two figures cannot both hold. Neither figure is sourced.", "Print-on-demand internal contradiction: 'roughly $1 per active design per month' then '100 designs, $300 a month'. At $1 per design, 100 designs is $100 a month; '500 designs ... $3,000 to $5,000 a month' implies $6 to $10 per design (analyst calculation). The stated '15 to 25% of designs generate any sales' further lowers the per-design average. Unsourced.", "Tax thesis: 'If that same dollar came from a dividend, a long-term investment, or rental income stream, it gets taxed at a completely different, much lower, rate' and 'every additional one gets more favorable treatment' is wrong for most of the video's own list. Net rental income, high-yield savings interest, T-bill and I bond interest (federally), most REIT dividends (the script itself says so), Groundfloor interest, vending, ATM, car wash and laundromat profits, Etsy, print-on-demand, stock royalties and focus-group pay are taxed as ordinary income, and self-employment business income adds 15.3% self-employment tax. Only qualified dividends and long-term capital gains get the 0 to 20% rates (plus the 3.8% net investment income tax at higher incomes, not mentioned). The script contradicts its own thesis at item 9.", "'The Forbes 400 wealthiest Americans paid an average effective tax rate of 8.2%': matches the 2021 White House CEA/OMB estimate of an 8.2% average federal individual income tax rate for the 400 wealthiest families over 2010 to 2018, available well before publication. The narrator omits that the estimate counts unrealized gains on unsold assets as income, which is why it is low; the comparison 'most middle-class workers pay more than double that' is not sourced and mixes income tax with the payroll tax mentioned earlier.", "'The IRS data on millionaires shows they average seven income streams' (repeated in the close): no primary IRS document was found; search results show only secondary commentary repeating the figure and articles describing it as a myth with an unclear origin. Treat as UNVERIFIED and likely misattributed; the causal explanation ('because the first income stream gets taxed the hardest') is narrator opinion.", "Take-home on $58,000: analyst calculation for a single 2026 filer (standard deduction $16,100; 10% and 12% brackets) gives federal income tax about $4,780 plus 7.65% FICA about $4,437, leaving about $48,783 before state tax; a 5% to 6% state income tax brings that near $45,500 to $46,000, so the narrator's $42,000 to $46,000 range is plausible for income-tax states but understates no-income-tax states. The hook's '$3,400 a month after taxes' ($40,800 a year) falls below the narrator's own range unless pre-tax benefits or retirement contributions are deducted; minor internal inconsistency. 2026 bracket parameters are analyst knowledge, not re-fetched.", "I bonds '4.03% composite': consistent with the November 2025 to April 2026 rate (0.90% fixed, 4.03% composite) shown on TreasuryDirect's rate page and secondary coverage, current at the 2026-04-12 publication. Omitted: the $10,000 annual electronic purchase limit, a 12-month lockup and a 3-month interest penalty before five years. The state and local tax exemption for T-bill and I bond interest is correct.", "High-yield savings: '0.39% national average' and '10 to 12 times more' (implying about 3.9% to 4.7%) are plausible but were not checked against the FDIC national-rate page for April 2026; UNVERIFIED. '$10,000 ... could earn enough to cover a utility bill or your internet every month': at 4.2%, $10,000 earns about $35 a month (analyst calculation), below most monthly internet bills; overstated.", "SCHD: '$10,000 generates roughly $340 a year, $28 a month' is correct at 3.4% ($28.33). '$340 this year becomes $378 next year' implies 11.2% growth; '$800 annually' in 10 years requires about 9% annual dividend growth without reinvestment ($805) or more with reinvestment ($1,232 at 10% growth plus 3.4% reinvestment; analyst calculations). Yield, the 12% ten-year return and the 10 to 14% dividend growth rate were not verified for April 2026; past growth is not a forecast.", "REITs: the 90% taxable-income distribution requirement is correct. 'VNQ ... across 150 plus commercial properties' likely conflates holdings with properties: the fund holds REIT securities (each owning many properties); not verified here. 'Hold them inside a Roth IRA or 401k, and the tax drag disappears entirely' is true for Roth accounts but not for a traditional 401(k), where withdrawals are taxed as ordinary income (tax deferred, not removed).", "Car wash: 'Self-service locations generate $50,000 to $100,000 in annual revenue at 14.6% net margins' implies $7,300 to $14,600 a year net, about $608 to $1,217 a month (analyst calculation), after buying a location from about $50,000; this is far from 'more than your job'. The KKR $850 million Quick Quack investment concerns an express-tunnel subscription chain, not self-service bays; the deal amount was not verified here.", "Laundromat: '94.8% five-year survival' versus 'Restaurants sit around 60%' is unsourced and UNVERIFIED (the 94.8% figure appears to come from industry marketing, not BLS business-survival data). SBA arithmetic is internally consistent: 10% down of $32,000 implies a $320,000 purchase, and $80,000 a year on $320,000 is 25%, within the stated 20 to 28% ROI. But on a $288,000 loan at an assumed 10% over 10 years, debt service is about $3,806 a month or $45,671 a year (analyst calculation, rate and term assumed), leaving about $34,329 before owner labor, repairs and taxes if $80,000 is profit; if $80,000 is revenue the deal does not cover debt service. 'Dave Mens' is probably an ASR rendering of the Laundromat Millionaire's name; not corrected. '$2 million annually' is unclear between revenue and earnings; UNVERIFIED.", "Etsy '164,000 digital shops ... average monthly revenue of $2,444, median profit around $489', 'Rachel Jimenez hit $110,000', 'Amoro Designs earns $93,00